1. Stack (Automatic & Runtime)
When: Memory is allocated on the stack at runtime when a function is called and automatically deallocated when the function returns.
What: It stores local variables, function parameters, and return addresses.
Why it's confused with "Compile Time": The size of the stack frame (how much memory is needed) for a function is often calculated by the compiler, but the actual allocation happens at runtime.
Key Traits: Very fast, limited in size, Last-In-First-Out (LIFO). 
